description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/substrate-gap.ts, add a guard that stops a collapsing write from destroying a gap's stored problem statement.

This is a single replacement in one file. The text below occurs exactly once, so it is a safe anchor.

Replace this line:

  gap.created_at = cleanTs(existing.created_at, gap.created_at ?? now);

with these lines:

  gap.created_at = cleanTs(existing.created_at, gap.created_at ?? now);
  // A COLLAPSING SUMMARY IS AN ECHO, NOT A REWRITE. The guard below this one catches only
  // an ABSENT summary. A closing or edit goal is generated as `Close substrate gap <id>:
  // <summary, truncated>`, and the writer echoes that goal text back as the new summary, so
  // the incoming value is a LEADING FRAGMENT of the text it destroys. Observed three times
  // on 2026-09-05 against operator gaps: 4781 chars -> 11, 4519 -> 62, 3300 -> 123, each a
  // case-insensitive prefix of what it replaced, taking the gap's falsifier and measurements
  // with it. Because this store REPLACES rather than merges, that erases the very evidence
  // by which a false closure could be detected.
  if (
    typeof gap.summary === "string" &&
    typeof existing.summary === "string" &&
    gap.summary.trim().length > 0 &&
    gap.summary.trim().length < existing.summary.trim().length &&
    existing.summary.trim().toLowerCase().startsWith(gap.summary.trim().toLowerCase())
  ) {
    gap.summary = existing.summary;
  }

IMPORTANT ANCHORING NOTE: do NOT anchor on the line `if (typeof gap.summary !== "string" || gap.summary.length === 0) gap.summary = existing.summary;`. That line appears TWICE in this file and is therefore ambiguous. A previous attempt at this change was correctly refused by the applier with `no_unique_anchor` for exactly that reason. Anchor only on the `gap.created_at = cleanTs(...)` line quoted above, which is unique, and leave both copies of the summary guard untouched.

Ordering does not matter: the new block runs before the existing empty-summary guard, and the two do not interfere. An empty incoming summary fails the new block's `length > 0` test and falls through to the existing guard unchanged.

WHY THIS IS A BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, NOT A COMMENT: it changes which value is persisted. Today a write whose summary is a short leading fragment of the stored summary overwrites and destroys the longer text; afterwards the stored text is retained. The existing guard cannot cover this because it tests only for an empty or non-string summary, and all three observed destructions passed a non-empty string.

WHY A PREFIX TEST RATHER THAN A LENGTH RATIO: a genuine rewrite is essentially never a strict prefix of the text it replaces, so the false-positive surface is very small, and a shorter-but-different summary still replaces the old one as before.

